WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.760
How can a machine read DNA when a polymerase handles only the next base?

2
00:00:06.120 --> 00:00:08.120
Watch one blue template cross the frame.

3
00:00:09.300 --> 00:00:11.300
A gold copy grows beside it.

4
00:00:12.240 --> 00:00:14.720
The machine never sees an entire chromosome.

5
00:00:16.100 --> 00:00:19.260
It must turn local copying events into an ordered read.

6
00:00:22.733 --> 00:00:26.773
Sanger sequencing makes many copies from the same starting primer.

7
00:00:28.153 --> 00:00:31.473
Most copies take ordinary nucleotides and keep growing.

8
00:00:33.353 --> 00:00:36.113
Occasionally, a red dideoxynucleotide joins the copy.

9
00:00:37.353 --> 00:00:40.173
It lacks the usable end needed for another addition.

10
00:00:41.433 --> 00:00:46.413
That copy stops permanently, while other copies stop at other positions.

11
00:00:46.817 --> 00:00:49.877
The stopped copies now sort from shortest to longest.

12
00:00:51.277 --> 00:00:54.617
Each red endpoint carries the identity of its last base.

13
00:00:56.017 --> 00:00:59.717
Reading those endpoints in length order spells the newly made strand.

14
00:01:00.977 --> 00:01:06.197
The template runs in the opposite direction, so finding its sequence also requires

15
00:01:06.197 --> 00:01:07.417
reversing the complement.

16
00:01:09.150 --> 00:01:14.010
Now imagine replacing the permanent red stop with a removable teal pause.

17
00:01:15.270 --> 00:01:18.410
What must happen before polymerase can copy the next base?

18
00:01:24.417 --> 00:01:28.617
The machine images the colour while every copy in a cluster is paused.

19
00:01:29.897 --> 00:01:32.037
Then it removes both the dye and the block.

20
00:01:33.257 --> 00:01:35.717
The free end can accept another nucleotide.

21
00:01:37.137 --> 00:01:42.417
Repeating this add, image, and reset cycle reports one new base from every cluster

22
00:01:42.417 --> 00:01:43.417
each time.

23
00:01:44.050 --> 00:01:47.470
This is not Sanger sequencing performed in parallel.

24
00:01:49.010 --> 00:01:51.410
Sanger reads a permanent ladder of fragment lengths.

25
00:01:52.830 --> 00:01:56.730
Short-read sequencing follows temporary pauses through repeated cycles.

26
00:01:58.090 --> 00:02:02.650
Millions of clusters can work together, and both ends of each fragment can be read.

27
00:02:03.970 --> 00:02:06.050
Yet each reported stretch remains short.

28
00:02:07.100 --> 00:02:10.400
Long-read methods follow single molecules instead.

29
00:02:11.760 --> 00:02:16.940
In one method, a polymerase circles the same template repeatedly, letting repeated

30
00:02:16.940 --> 00:02:18.700
passes support a consensus.

31
00:02:20.280 --> 00:02:24.060
In the other, a strand moves through a pore while changing ionic current.

32
00:02:25.480 --> 00:02:27.900
Software converts that moving signal into bases.

33
00:02:29.480 --> 00:02:31.920
Both approaches preserve longer-range context.

34
00:02:33.033 --> 00:02:35.733
These methods do not merely differ in speed.

35
00:02:36.993 --> 00:02:42.073
The chemistry, the DNA fragments loaded, and base-calling software together shape

36
00:02:42.073 --> 00:02:44.593
read length, error pattern, and context.

37
00:02:45.993 --> 00:02:49.233
A read is still evidence from one fragment, not a chromosome.

38
00:02:50.753 --> 00:02:53.333
Later analysis must decide where it belongs.

39
00:02:54.893 --> 00:02:58.693
Genome results depend on what each read records and where it fits.

40
00:02:59.767 --> 00:03:03.647
Sequencing turns molecular events into ordered base calls.

41
00:03:04.847 --> 00:03:09.987
A polymerase stop, a cycle of coloured pauses, or a changing current becomes an

42
00:03:09.987 --> 00:03:14.927
ordered string. The chemistry shapes what evidence reaches the computer.

43
00:03:16.167 --> 00:03:21.147
That distinction will guide every calculation and genome comparison that follows.

44
00:03:22.387 --> 00:03:26.807
The next question is whether those strings fit one genomic path or several.
